1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,912
Hello World, and welcome to Episode 1 of pg_bio!

2
00:00:03,912 --> 00:00:07,824
If you are a software or database engineer, biology can look like a wall

3
00:00:07,824 --> 00:00:11,736
of jargon. But the Central Dogma of biology is literally a compiler:

4
00:00:11,736 --> 00:00:15,648
DNA is 4-letter source code on disk, RNA is an ephemeral message queue, and

5
00:00:15,648 --> 00:00:19,560
proteins are compiled 3D state machines.

6
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:24,680
In traditional bioinformatics, engineers export gigabytes of FASTA and VCF files to Python just

7
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:29,800
to translate codons or check genomic intervals. With pg_bio, that entire compiler runs natively

8
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:34,920
inside PostgreSQL shared buffers in Rust.

9
00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:40,496
Here is your first Systems-to-Biotech bridge: a human chromosome is just a 1D integer

10
00:00:40,496 --> 00:00:46,072
coordinate space. Using PostgreSQL int4range and GiST indexes, finding a CRISPR safe-harbor locus near

11
00:00:46,072 --> 00:00:51,648
a promoter without touching a tumor suppressor gene is a sub-millisecond interval query.

12
00:00:51,648 --> 00:00:59,448
Look at psql: in one query, bio_translate_dna compiles DNA codons into amino acids, isoelectric_point

13
00:00:59,448 --> 00:01:07,248
runs 100-step Henderson-Hasselbalch bisection to find net charge, and gravy_score measures water-repellency in 200 nanoseconds.

14
00:01:07,248 --> 00:01:13,236
You just executed genomics and protein sequence physics in 0.2 milliseconds inside SQL.

15
00:01:13,236 --> 00:01:19,224
Explore all articles at ideia dot me and check out the pg_bio repository on GitHub.
